@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# VLA-0
+
+LeRobot can deploy [VLA-0](https://github.com/NVlabs/vla0) checkpoints through the HTTP inference service in [mattpidden/vla0](https://github.com/mattpidden/vla0). The model and its CUDA dependencies stay in the VLA-0 environment, while `lerobot-rollout` owns camera capture, robot control, recording, task prompts, and rollout strategies.
+
+This adapter is inference-only and currently matches the fork's SO-100/SO-101 service contract: two RGB cameras, six joint positions in degrees, and six absolute joint-position actions.
+
+## Start the VLA-0 server
+
+Install and test the VLA-0 fork in its own environment, then start its service with your checkpoint:
+
+```bash
+cd /path/to/vla0
+ROBOVERSE_DEPLOY_CHECKPOINT=/path/to/model_last.pth \
+    python rv_train/deploy/service.py
+```
+
+The service listens on port `10000` by default. Verify it before connecting a robot:
+
+```bash
+curl http://127.0.0.1:10000/health
+```
+
+## Run with `lerobot-rollout`
+
+Camera names and order must match training. The fork's real-world client uses `wrist` first and `middle` second:
+
+```bash
+uv run lerobot-rollout \
+    --strategy.type=base \
+    --inference.type=sync \
+    --policy.type=vla0 \
+    --policy.server_url=http://127.0.0.1:10000 \
+    --policy.n_action_steps=4 \
+    --robot.type=so101_follower \
+    --robot.port=/dev/ttyACM1 \
+    --robot.id=blue_follower \
+    --robot.use_degrees=true \
+    --robot.max_relative_target=5 \
+    --robot.cameras="{wrist: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 0, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30}, middle: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 12, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30}}" \
+    --task="Pick up the juggling ball and place it in the bowl." \
+    --fps=10 \
+    --duration=60
+```
+
+No `--policy.path` is needed because the checkpoint is loaded by the server. `policy.n_action_steps` controls how many returned actions are executed before requesting a new chunk.
+
+The synchronous backend pauses the control loop while VLA-0 generates each chunk. To generate in a background thread and append complete chunks to the rollout queue, use:
+
+```bash
+--inference.type=rtc \
+--inference.rtc.enabled=false \
+--inference.queue_threshold=4
+```
+
+`rtc.enabled=false` is intentional: the VLA-0 service does not implement RTC prefix inpainting, so the asynchronous engine is used only as a producer/consumer queue.
+
+## Configuration
+
+| Flag | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `policy.server_url` | `http://127.0.0.1:10000` | VLA-0 service base URL |
+| `policy.camera_keys` | wrist, middle | Ordered LeRobot image feature keys |
+| `policy.n_action_steps` | `4` | Actions consumed from each returned chunk |
+| `policy.request_timeout_s` | `300` | Inference request timeout |
+| `policy.check_server_on_load` | `true` | Check `/health` before connecting the robot |
+
+Keep `robot.use_degrees=true`: the fork's real-world data and client represent SO-101 state and actions in degrees. Keep a conservative `robot.max_relative_target` during initial tests, begin with a short `duration`, and be ready to stop the robot.
